The choice between BCS and mastectomy often remains a complex and multifaceted decision for breast surgeons. This decision can be influenced by several patient, tumor, and histopathological features. Understanding these factors is crucial for optimizing treatment strategies and aligning surgical decisions with the latest clinical evidence. We retrospectively collected and analyzed data from BC patients treated in two Italian Breast Units who achieved a complete response on the primary tumor (i.e.ypT0) after NAT and were subsequently treated with BCS or mastectomy. The aim of this study is to identify factors influencing surgical decision-making and predicting the use of mastectomy despite the tumor’s complete response to NAT.
2. Materials and Methods
2.1. Study design and participants
This study is a pooled analysis from DataBreast, a prospectively maintained European Society of BC Specialist (EUSOMA)-approved institutional database. Patients informed consent was obtained before performing data entry. We collected data from all consecutive BC patients treated with NAT followed by surgery at the Breast Unit of Humanitas Istituto Clinico Catanese (HICC, Catania, Italy), and IRCCS Humanitas Research Hospital (ICH, Rozzano, Italy) between 2009 and 2022. Only patients with a documented pathologic complete response of the primary tumor (ypT0), defined as the absence of residual invasive or in-situ carcinoma on the surgical specimen obtained after NAT were eligible for inclusion. Pathological nodal status could be either ypN0 (i.e., pathologic complete response, pCR) or ypN+. Patients with known pathogenic germline BRCA1/2 variants and those presenting with metastatic disease (cM+) at the initial diagnosis were excluded from the study. Data on age and menopausal status at diagnosis, tumor site, focality, dimensions at baseline ultrasound (US) and MRI, primary tumor stage, clinical nodal status, tumor grade, molecular features, and intrinsic subtype, type of NAT regimens, and NAT discontinuation were collected. Pre-operative staging involved bilateral mammogram, breast, and axillary US in the majority of patients. While pre- and post-operative MRI of the breast was not mandatory initially, its frequency increased during the study period as later internal guidelines recommended it for loco-regional staging post-NAT. The evaluation of hormone receptor status was conducted through immunohistochemistry (IHC). Estrogen (ER) and progesterone receptor (PgR) status was defined as positive when their expression was observed in at least 1 % of immunoreactive cells [21]. The assessment of human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2) status involved both IHC and fluorescent in-situ hybridization (FISH) techniques, following the ASCO-CAP guidelines [22]. To monitor the response to systemic treatment, patients underwent post-NAT evaluation using one or a combination of the following: bilateral breast and axillary US, MRI of the breast, or positron emission tomography-computed tomography (PET/CT). Management of each BC patient was discussed three times at multidisciplinary tumor board meetings, which included breast/oncoplastic surgeons, medical oncologists, pathologists, radiation oncologists, and radiologists. The first multidisciplinary tumor board meeting was held before initiating NAT, during which the decision was made to proceed with NAT followed by surgery; however, the type of breast surgery (BCS or mastectomy) was not specified at this time. The second multidisciplinary meeting took place after completion of NAT and before surgery to evaluate tumor response. The third and final multidisciplinary tumor board meeting was held after surgery to discuss pathological findings and plan adjuvant treatments.
2.2. Statistical analysis
Patients were divided into two groups according to the surgical procedure performed: BCS or mastectomy. Descriptive statistics were used for cohort characteristics. Continuous variables were summarized
with median and interquartile range (IQR), and the Wilcoxon rank-sum test was employed for comparison. Binary and categorical variables were summarized using proportions. Two-sample tests of proportions and the Chi square test for heterogeneity were used for comparison, respectively. Univariate logistic regression was performed; variables with a significant correlation were then tested in a multivariate model. Distant recurrence-free survival (DRFS), defined as time from breast surgery to first occurrence of relapse in a distant site, and event-free survival (EFS), defined as time from breast surgery to first occurrence of relapse at any site or death, were evaluated. The Kaplan-Maier method was used for curves visualization and the log-rank test to define significant differences in survival between patients treated with BCS or mastectomy. For all statistical tests, the significance level was set at 0.05. All analyses were conducted using STATA v.18 (StataCorp. 2023. Stata Statistical Software: Release 18. College Station, TX: StataCorp LLC).
3. Results
3.1. Population characteristics
Overall, 1407 BC patients were treated with NAT in the two breast units during the considered study period (2009–2022). Of these, 243 Caucasian women who achieved ypT0 were included, 147 (60.5 %) undergoing BCS and 96 (39.5 %) undergoing mastectomy. The distribution of surgical types was balanced across two hospital sites (p = 0.87). The median age was 50 years (IQR: 44–58), and menopausal status was similarly distributed (p = 0.46) between both groups. The upper external quadrant was the most common tumor site for both BCS and mastectomy patients. However, a notable prevalence of centrallylocated cancers was observed in the mastectomy group (30.2 % vs 19.7 % in the BCS group, p = 0.009). Multiple foci on initial US were more frequent in the mastectomy group (33.3 % vs 16.4 % in the BCS group; p = 0.001), and tumors in this group were larger at baseline US (median extension 29 mm vs 24 mm in the BCS group; p = 0.009). Baseline MRI was performed in 63.4 % of patients, without significant differences between groups (p = 0.97). Mastectomy patients had more frequently multiple foci on MRI (42.7 % vs 27.2 % in BCS; p = 0.005) and larger tumor dimensions (median extension 40 mm vs 30 mm in BCS; p = 0.0001). The primary tumor stage (cT stage) showed a higher proportion of cT3/4 cancers in the mastectomy group (18.8 % vs 6.8 % in BCS; p = 0.003), leading to a greater number of stage III BC cases (14.6 % vs 6.1 % in BCS; p = 0.03). No significant differences were observed in tumor grading, hormone receptor status, HER2 status, Ki67 index, and intrinsic molecular subtype between groups. Chemotherapy regimens, primarily sequential anthracycline and taxane, was similar across groups (p = 0.73), as was the use of targeted agents (58.4 %, p = 0.58). Chemotherapy discontinuation rates were comparable (10.3 %, p = 0.71). Descriptive statistics are summarized in Table 1.
3.2. Regression analysis
In order to identify potential predictors of mastectomy in our population, we conducted univariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses. In the univariate analysis, several factors emerged as significantly related to the likelihood of receiving a mastectomy. Patients with multiple tumor foci at baseline US were more likely to undergo mastectomy, with an odds ratio (OR) of 2.69 [95 % confidence interval (95 % CI): 1.45–4.99, p = 0.002]. The cancer extension at baseline US was also a significant predictor, with each millimeter increase in tumor size associated with a 3 % increase in the likelihood of mastectomy (OR: 1.03, 95 % CI: 1.01–1.04, p = 0.008). Similarly, the tumor’s focality and size at baseline MRI influenced the surgical decision: multiple foci were associated with a higher probability of mastectomy (OR: 2.59, 95 % CI: 1.33–5.04, p = 0.005), while each millimeter increase correlated with a 5 % higher chance of mastectomy (OR: 1.05, 95 % CI: 1.02–1.07, p <
