0.0001). Patients with cT3-4 tumors had significantly higher odds of mastectomy compared to those with smaller tumors (OR: 5.52, 95 % CI: 1.91–16.01, p = 0.001). Additionally, patients with TNM stage III disease were more likely to undergo mastectomy compared to those with stages I or II (OR: 4.32, 95 % CI: 1.39–13.4, p = 0.01). Univariate analysis results are summarized in Table 2. In a multivariate regression model that included variables with a statistically significant correlation in the univariate analysis (i.e., baseline US focality, baseline US extension, baseline MRI focality, baseline MRI extension, primary tumor’s stage, and TNM tumor’s stage), only US focality and MRI extension retained their significance (OR 2.69, 95 % CI: 1.04–6.92; p = 0.041 and OR 1.04, 95 % CI: 1.01–1.09; p = 0.015, respectively, Pseudo R2 0.16) (Table 3).
3.3. Survival analysis
Median follow-up was 38.9 months in the BCS group (IQR 23.2–57.6 months) and 38.4 months in the mastectomy group (IQR 23.1–68.5 months). Overall, 21 patients (8.6 %) had a distant recurrence, 8 (5.4 %) in the BCS group and 13 in the mastectomy group (13.5 %). At 12, 24 and 36 months DRFS was 96.5 %, 95.0 % and 94.0 % among patients treated with BCS and 95.7 %, 89.4 % and 86.4 % among patients treated with mastectomy, respectively. Distant-recurrence free survival difference was significant between the two groups (log-rank p = 0.031) (Fig. 1A).
