4. Discussion
The use of NAT in BC has substantially increased over the past years
[13,23]. Pre-operative systemic treatment may lead to conversion from mastectomy to BCS, especially when breast conservation was not initially feasible. This is particularly true for BC patients who achieve complete remission of the primary tumor (i.e., ypT0). Even though a considerable number of eligible patients undergo BCS following NAT, mastectomy is still performed in many cases. In recent neo-adjuvant trials, low rates of breast conservation have been observed despite the increase of pCR rates with innovative therapies [14,18,19,24–28]. A meta-analysis of 36 studies involving 12,311 BC patients confirmed that pCR does not increase BCS in patients receiving NAT [29]. We conducted a retrospective analysis of BC patients with ypT0 treated in two Italian Breast Units, to assess factors influencing surgical decision-making and identify predictors of mastectomy following NAT in this population. Data were extracted from a standardized, highquality database, making our results comparable and widely applicable across the network that utilizes DataBreast. According to regression analysis, cancer extension and focality on baseline US and MRI significantly correlated with the post-NAT surgical choice. Pre-operative radiological assessment after NAT is more challenging compared to the upfront surgical setting. Tumor histopathological and biological features, heterogeneous response patterns, and potential inconsistencies between clinical and pathological responses can all contribute to the lack of confidence in post-NAT radiological evaluations [30–32]. Additionally, general consensus about the optimal radiological work-up after NAT has not been achieved yet [33–35]. Keune et al. conducted a retrospective analysis of 192 BC patients with 196 primary tumors and reported that US was more accurate than mammography in measuring residual disease after NAT. Ultrasound accurately sized the residual disease in 91.3 % of cases, compared to 51.9 % measured by mammography (p < 0.0001) [36]. Sudhir et al. compared post-NAT mammography, US, and MRI in 56 consecutive stage II-III BC patients [37]. Accuracy rates for predicting residual tumor size were 57 % for mammography, 50 % for US and 85 % for MRI. The sensitivity of MRI to detect pCR was 72.7 % [37]. A meta-analysis of 44 studies conducted between 1990 and 2008, involving 2050 BC patients who underwent imaging evaluation of residual disease after NAT, found that MRI exhibited generally high sensitivity (83–87 %) and variable specificity (54–83 %). However, no differences between MRI and US were found in terms of accuracy (p = 0.15) [38]. In a systematic review comprising 35 studies, the sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, and negative predictive value of MRI to predict pCR ranged from 25 % to 100 %, 50 %–97 %, 47 %–73 %, and 71 %–100 %, respectively [39]. A retrospective study by Kuzmova et al. demonstrated an overall accuracy of 77.3 % for MRI in predicting pCR, with significantly better performance in triple-negative BC compared to hormone
