receptor-positive/HER2-negative sub-type (p = 0.0057) [40]. Given its accuracy, MRI is considered the standard tool for primary tumor assessment after NAT [39,41,42]. However, it may overestimate the extent of residual disease, eventually leading to radical surgery and poorer cosmetic outcomes even when BCS is technically feasible and clinically appropriate [43–45]. Marinovich et al. [46] conducted a meta-analysis of 19 studies, examining the agreement between MRI and pathology in this setting. The study showed a tendency for MRI to overestimate pathologic tumor size, with a pooled mean difference of 0.1 cm. According to a retrospective study by Kwak et al. including 225 BC patients who underwent a MRI before and after NAT between 2016 and 2021, 15 % of the results were false positives [47]. Multiple tumor foci at baseline assessment was another factor significantly correlating with the choice of mastectomy in our study. Surgical decision-making in BC patients with more than two lesions in a single quadrant (i.e.multifocal) or across multiple quadrants (i.e.multicentric) often involves complex considerations and a degree of uncertainty for both patients and breast surgeons, especially after NAT. Guidelines usually indicate mastectomy for BC patients with unclear pre-operative margin status, including multifocal/centric presentations [48]. These recommendations are partly based on previous evidence showing poorer prognosis in multifocal/multicentric disease [49–51]. However, a recent pooled analysis of three prospective trials involving 6134 patients undergoing BCS after NAT showed that local control and OS are not inferior in BC patients with multicentric/multifocal tumors compared to those with unifocal presentation, provided that clear surgical margins are achieved [52]. Tumor shrinkage after NAT poses a challenge for breast surgeons that must accurately locating and completely excising the primary tumor site. Still, the use of metallic clip to mark the tumor bed in BC patients potentially candidate to BCS should be routing practice before NAT [53, 54]. The metallic clip remains detectable during the entire course of NAT, facilitating pre-operative localization and intra-operative identification of the tumor bed [55]. In the upfront surgical setting, BCS has proven to be a safe alternative to mastectomy in terms of OS [56,57], with recent studies even reporting improved survival with BCS [58,59]. Similar results were obtained in patients undergoing BCS after NAT, showing that breast conservation is not detrimental compared to mastectomy, while providing better cosmetic outcomes and quality of life [20,60,61]. However, fear of local recurrence may represent another driver of mastectomy choice in patients with tumor complete response after NAT. This concern was accentuated by findings from a meta-analysis by the Early BC Trialists’ Collaborative Group (EBCTCG) in 2018 [62], including ten randomized trials with a median follow-up of nine years, indicated a higher incidence of local recurrences in patients who
underwent BCS for NAT-downsized tumors, compared to those receiving BCS in the adjuvant setting for similarly sized tumors. Notably, within these trials, two studies did not conduct surgical intervention post-NAT in cases of clinical complete response. The likelihood of local recurrence was particularly elevated in those initially designated for mastectomy but who ultimately received BCS, highlighting potential issues in surgical planning (i.e. radiological imaging and pre-operative tumor localization). Moreover, data on axillary surgery and radiotherapy were unknown. On the other hand, Werutsky et al. performed a pooled analysis including 10,075 patients with primary BC after NAT, showing that the 5-year cumulative loco-regional recurrence rates were 7.8 % among patients treated with BCS and 11.3 % with mastectomy [63]. In the recent prospective, randomized, I-SPY2 trial, Mukhtar et al. evaluated the relationship between the response to NAT, as measured by the residual cancer burden (RCB) method, and the loco-regional recurrence rate in 1462 BC patients who underwent surgical treatment (BCS vs mastectomy) between the years 2010 and 2021 [64]. At a median follow-up of 3.5 years, 34 (5.4 %) patients in the BCS group and 58 (7.0 %) patients in the mastectomy group experienced a loco-regional recurrence (p = 0.18). Those patients with RCB 2/3 disease after NAT experienced a significantly shorter loco-regional free survival than those with RCB 0/1 disease, regardless of whether BCS or mastectomy was performed. Among patients with RCB 0/1 status after NAT, there was no difference in loco-regional free survival between those having BCS vs mastectomy. Additionally, many other studies have consistently demonstrated that BCS does not compromise local recurrence rates or OS in BC patients treated with NAT [17–19]. Results of survival analysis in our cohort are in line with previous findings, demonstrating that BCS is not detrimental compared to mastectomy in patients responding to NAT [65–69]. Despite in our study DRFS and EFS are significantly better in patients undergoing BCS, these findings should be interpreted cautiously, due to the unbalance in baseline patients characteristics (e.g. more advanced stage in the mastectomy group) and lack of control for potential post-NAT biases (e.g. adjuvant treatments received). Limitations of our study include its retrospective design, the lack of information about patient preferences and individual concerns, and the absence of post-NAT ultrasound data. However, we have implemented measures to mitigate them. To overcome the lack of post-NAT US data, we included post-NAT MRI assessments in our analysis, which provided comprehensive imaging information. By focusing on a homogeneous group of BC patients who achieved ypT0 after NAT and underwent surgery across two breast units, our study ensures a consistent treatment approach. The use of data from a standardized, high-quality database enhances the reliability of our findings. Although the study does not encompass considerations about oncoplastic procedure and delayed reconstruction, its structured approach and the specific patient cohort
