enhance the generalizability and relevance of its results to other breast units. Avoiding unnecessary mastectomies may enhance patient quality of life while safeguarding clinical outcomes. Multidisciplinary management and rigorous evaluation of patient and tumor characteristics before and after NAT is paramount for optimizing the treatment of these women. Our retrospective data suggest that mastectomy in patients with ypT0 disease post-NAT does not provide any advantage in terms of locoregional staging and long-term survival outcomes. Indeed, BCS may yield oncological outcomes that are comparable, if not superior, to those following mastectomy.
5. Conclusions
In conclusion, BCS should be prioritized whenever feasible to improve the management of BC patients responding to NAT. Prospective studies are needed to confirm our findings in the future.
